course: chemistry
lesson: 21
title: Bread rising: yeast at work
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mum leaves a bowl of bread dough by the radiator, and an hour later it has puffed up to twice its size. The dough is full of yeast, a tiny living fungus, which eats sugar and breathes out carbon dioxide gas.
explain: Kneading pulls the dough's gluten proteins into long stretchy strands, like a net of tiny balloons. They trap the gas, so the dough swells, and every hole in a slice of bread is where a bubble once was.
explain: In the hot oven the outside browns into a crust, as sugars and proteins react with heat, while the inside stays soft because its water keeps it below 100 °C.
explain: Not all bread needs yeast: pitta and tortillas are flat, and pitta puffs up only because water inside turns to steam. Cakes rise with baking powder or whisked eggs instead. And yeast has a cousin nobody invited: the fluffy mould on old bread.
fact: Yeast is a tiny living fungus; in dough it eats sugar and gives off carbon dioxide gas that puffs the dough up.
fact: Warmth makes yeast work faster, so dough is left somewhere warm; in a cold fridge it rises very slowly, which bakers use to make it tastier.
fact: Kneading lines up gluten proteins into long stretchy strands that trap the gas like balloons.
fact: The holes in bread are where bubbles of gas were trapped; bread without gas is flat and dense.
fact: Stretchy gluten is why pizza dough can be spun and stretched thin without tearing.
fact: Flatbreads like pitta and tortilla are made without yeast; pitta gets its pocket because water inside turns to steam in a hot oven.
fact: Yeast in warm sugary water wakes up, eats the sugar and foams; water that is too hot kills yeast.
fact: Sourdough uses wild yeast and bacteria; the bacteria make acids that give the sour taste, and a starter can be kept alive and fed for many years.
fact: Bread goes stale as its starch slowly lines up and squeezes out water; warming it in the oven softens it for a while.
fact: The crust browns as sugars and proteins react with heat; the inside stays soft and pale because its water keeps it below 100 °C.
fact: Cakes rise without yeast, using baking powder or whisked eggs to trap gas that expands in the oven heat.
fact: A soufflé sinks soon after leaving the oven because the air inside cools and shrinks.
fact: Mould is a fungus related to yeast that grows from spores on damp bread; penicillin, a life-saving medicine, was first found from a mould, but mould on bread can be harmful, so mouldy bread goes in the bin.
exercise: What is yeast? | A tiny living fungus | A very finely ground flour | A type of salt | A small seed
exercise: What gas makes bread dough rise? | Carbon dioxide | Pure oxygen gas | Helium | Steam only
exercise: Why do recipes say to leave dough somewhere warm? | Warmth makes yeast work faster | Warmth melts the flour together | Cold dough turns into cake | Warmth adds sugar to the dough
exercise: What does kneading do to dough? | Lines up gluten into stretchy strands | Squeezes all the gas out of it for good | Kills the yeast so it stops | Turns the flour into sugar
exercise: What are the holes in a slice of bread? | Places where gas bubbles were trapped | Holes poked in by the baker's fork before baking | Spaces left by melted butter | Tunnels dug by the yeast
exercise: Why can pizza dough be stretched very thin? | Stretchy gluten strands hold it together | It is made with lots and lots of olive oil | Pizza dough has no flour in it | It is frozen before stretching
exercise: Why does pitta bread have a pocket inside? | Water inside turns to steam in the hot oven | The baker cuts a hole in the side of each one | Yeast makes a giant bubble | It is baked around a balloon
exercise: Why are tortillas flat? | They are made without yeast | They are squashed after baking | They are baked too long | They have too much salt
exercise: What would happen to yeast in boiling water? | It would be killed | It would foam more than ever | It would turn into sugar | It would start to glow
exercise: Why does sourdough taste sour? | Bacteria in it make acids | Lemon juice is added to the dough | It is baked in vinegar | The flour has gone off
exercise: How can a sourdough starter be older than the baker using it? | It can be kept alive and fed for years | It is dug up from the cellars of old bakeries | It never needs any food | It is bought fresh every day
exercise: Why does bread go stale? | Starch slowly lines up and squeezes out water | The yeast inside goes to sleep and stops holding it up | Air puts new sugar into it | The crust soaks up the inside
exercise: Why is the crust brown but the inside white? | The inside's water keeps it below 100 °C | The crust is made of a different flour | The inside is coated in milk | Brown flour sinks to the outside
exercise: How does a cake rise without yeast? | Baking powder or whisked eggs give gas | It is pumped full of helium from a tank | It is left in the fridge overnight | The sugar puffs up on its own
exercise: Why does a soufflé sink after leaving the oven? | The air inside cools and shrinks | The eggs start to rot | The yeast runs out of sugar | Cold air pushes it down from above
exercise: How is bread mould related to yeast? | Both are fungi | Mould is a kind of flour | Mould is yeast that got cold | They are not related at all
test: Ollie's dough is in the fridge and hardly rising. His sister says the yeast is dead. What's more likely? | It's just working slowly in the cold | Yes, fridges kill all yeast at once | The flour has turned to salt | Dough never rises in a fridge
test: "used water straight from the kettle for my yeast and nothing happened lol". Why? | Water that's too hot kills yeast | The yeast needed salt, not sugar | Kettle water has no oxygen | The yeast was asleep, wait a week
test: If you made bread dough but forgot to knead it at all, what would the bread be like? | Flatter and more crumbly | Bigger and fluffier than ever | Exactly the same | Bright brown inside
test: A cartoon shows a baker blowing into dough with a straw to make the holes. What really makes them? | Gas from the yeast | The baker's breath | Air from the oven fan | Drops of water
test: Gran: "stale bread? pop it in the oven". Why does that work? | Warming softens it for a while | The oven adds fresh yeast | The oven puts sugar back | Heat turns the bread back into dough
test: Leo says penicillin proves all mould is good to eat. What's true? | One mould gave a medicine, but bread mould can be harmful | All mould is a medicine, so any mouldy bread is healthy | Penicillin is made from yeast, not mould | Mould is never useful for anything
test: Which of these rises without any yeast? | A sponge cake with whisked eggs | A loaf of sourdough | A white sandwich loaf | A bread roll
test: "how's the soufflé gone flat already, it looked amazing 2 mins ago??" | The air inside cooled and shrank | Someone poked it with a fork | The eggs went off in the oven | The yeast died in the cold
test: You put yeast in warm sugary water and it foams and smells bready. What has happened? | The yeast woke up and is eating the sugar | The sugar has started boiling in the warm water | The water has gone off | The yeast has turned into flour
test: Why does the middle of a loaf stay soft when the crust gets crunchy? | Its water keeps it below 100 °C | The oven heat stops at the crust | The middle is made of cake | Gluten stops all heat getting in
test: "pitta's got no yeast so how's it got a pocket??" | Steam from water inside puffs it up | It's cut open by a machine | It's baked folded in half | Bakers blow it up like a balloon
exam: Identify the organism principally responsible for the rising of bread dough. | Yeast, a microscopic fungus | A species of bacterium found in salt | A mould introduced by the oven | A virus present in flour
exam: It is given that dough is kneaded thoroughly prior to rising. Ascertain the principal effect of such kneading. | Gluten proteins are aligned into elastic strands which retain gas | All the gas is expelled permanently and the yeast is rendered inactive | The yeast is destroyed by friction | The starch is converted entirely into sugar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the staling of bread? | Starch gradually realigns and expels water | Yeast consumes the remaining bread | Mould converts the bread into stone | Air introduces additional water into the loaf
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rising of a cake prepared without yeast. | Baking powder or whisked eggs supply gas that expands in the heat | The cake rises through the action of mould spores | The cake rises because sugar becomes lighter than air | The cake cannot rise in the absence of yeast
